A degree, diploma or school certificate is often the first thing an employer checks. It shows you have reached a recognised standard. But once you are in the interview — or in the job — what matters is what you can actually do. That is why the strongest candidates combine a recognised qualification with practical skills.

## Why a qualification still matters

Many roles set a minimum qualification as a filter: a secondary certificate, a diploma or a bachelor's degree. Promotions and further study often depend on it too. Without the right qualification, a strong candidate can be screened out before anyone sees their abilities.

## Why skills make the difference

Two candidates with the same degree are rarely equal. The one who can build a clear report in Excel, present confidently in English or use AI tools to work faster usually stands out — and settles into the job more quickly.

Skills that help in almost every role include:

- **Communication in English** — emails, meetings, presentations and customer conversations
- **Spreadsheets and reporting** — Excel, and dashboards in tools such as Power BI
- **AI tools** — using AI assistants safely to draft, summarise and analyse
- **Digital skills** — social media, design basics and online marketing
- **Project and time management** — planning work and delivering on time

## How to build both at once

You do not have to finish one before starting the other. Common combinations include:

1. **Finishing school + Business English or Excel** — strengthens your CV while you complete Grade 10 or 12.
2. **Starting a degree + IELTS or Python** — meet entry requirements and get a head start in tech.
3. **Completing a degree + Power BI or project management** — skills managers value when you graduate.
4. **Working professional + ACCA, CMA or Excel with AI** — a recognised qualification plus time-saving tools.

## Plan around the job you want

Start from your goal. Which qualification does that job require? Which skills do people in that role use every day? Then build a plan that covers both, in an order that fits your time and budget.

Use the [qualification and skills planner](/#planner) on our homepage to see suggested combinations, or [talk to an advisor](/contact) for a personal plan.
